<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n22664" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">incursĭto</span></b> , āre,
			<div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>v. freq.
			n.</i> [incurso], <i>to rush upon</i>, <i>assault</i>, <i>attack</i> (a
			favorite word of Seneca). </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">in aliquem,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Sen. Vit. Beat. 27</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">incursitans, instans, fugans, id. de Ira, 2,
					35: in multos,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					ib. 3, 6</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>, <i>to dash against</i>,
				<i>clash with</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">totā vitā incursitamus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Sen.
					Ep. 110</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
